                  "end": 152,
                  "external_id": "GHSA-4qjh-9fv9-r85r",
                  "source_id": "github-advisories",
                  "origin": "live"
                },
                "text": "[{\"first_patched_version\": \"0.9.0\", \"package\": {\"ecosystem\": \"pip\", \"name\": \"vllm\"}, \"vulnerable_functions\": [], \"vulnerable_version_range\": \"< 0.9.0\"}]",
                "text_truncated": false
              },
              {
                "citation": {
                  "chunk_id": "23948aeeab5180c89cb54623",
                  "event_id": "1868db90-f569-426c-96d9-bf691a25088d",
                  "revision_id": "249beafe-bec3-4494-ab09-406fd96b85fd",
                  "revision": 1,
                  "raw_document_id": "6e452537-d696-4fa9-94c4-7ca8a27b2142",
                  "json_pointer": "/8/description",
                  "sha256": "97d404bc94da3609cd843125df913275f94cde6ba33f83e5175be86a3986665a",
                  "field": "description",
                  "start": 780,
                  "end": 1680,
                  "external_id": "GHSA-4qjh-9fv9-r85r",
                  "source_id": "github-advisories",
                  "origin": "live"
                },
                "text": "\nUnlike token-by-token sharing mechanisms, vLLM’s chunk-based approach (PageAttention) processes tokens in larger units (chunks). In our tests, with chunk_size=2, the timing differences became noticeable enough to allow attackers to infer whether portions of their input match the victim's prompt at the chunk level.\n\n## Environment\n\n- GPU: NVIDIA A100 (40G)\n- CUDA: 11.8\n- PyTorch: 2.3.1\n- OS: Ubuntu 18.04\n- vLLM: v0.5.1\nConfiguration: We launched vLLM using the default settings and adjusted chunk_size=2 to evaluate the TTFT.\n\n## Leakage\nWe conducted our tests using LLaMA2-70B-GPTQ on a single device. We analyzed the timing differences when prompts shared prefixes of 2 chunks, and plotted the ",
                "text_truncated": true
              },
              {
                "citation": {
                  "chunk_id": "522661997620ec1768f4b59a",
                  "event_id": "1868db90-f569-426c-96d9-bf691a25088d",
                  "revision_id": "249beafe-bec3-4494-ab09-406fd96b85fd",
                  "revision": 1,
                  "raw_document_id": "6e452537-d696-4fa9-94c4-7ca8a27b2142",
                  "json_pointer": "/8/description",
                  "sha256": "97d404bc94da3609cd843125df913275f94cde6ba33f83e5175be86a3986665a",
                  "field": "description",
                  "start": 1560,
                  "end": 2460,
                  "external_id": "GHSA-4qjh-9fv9-r85r",
                  "source_id": "github-advisories",
                  "origin": "live"
                },
                "text": "iably used to distinguish prefix matches, demonstrating a potential side-channel vulnerability.\n<img src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/db3491e9-02b7-424c-9b6d-56f553b39f2f\" alt=\"roc_curves_combined_block_2\" width=\"400\"/>\n\n\n## Results\nIn our experiment, we analyzed the response time differences between cache hits and misses in vLLM's PageAttention mechanism. Using ROC curve analysis to assess the distinguishability of these timing differences, we observed the following results:\n- With a 1-token prefix, the ROC curve yielded an AUC value of 0.571, indicating that even with a short prefix, an attacker can reasonably distinguish between cache hits and misses based on response times",
                "text_truncated": true
              },
              {
                "citation": {
                  "chunk_id": "7c75376d63e709d07da2b983",
                  "event_id": "1868db90-f569-426c-96d9-bf691a25088d",
                  "revision_id": "249beafe-bec3-4494-ab09-406fd96b85fd",
                  "revision": 1,
                  "raw_document_id": "6e452537-d696-4fa9-94c4-7ca8a27b2142",
                  "json_pointer": "/8/description",
                  "sha256": "97d404bc94da3609cd843125df913275f94cde6ba33f83e5175be86a3986665a",
                  "field": "description",
                  "start": 0,
                  "end": 900,
                  "external_id": "GHSA-4qjh-9fv9-r85r",
                  "source_id": "github-advisories",
                  "origin": "live"
                },
                "text": "This issue arises from the prefix caching mechanism, which may expose the system to a timing side-channel attack.\n\n## Description\nWhen a new prompt is processed, if the PageAttention mechanism finds a matching prefix chunk, the prefill process speeds up, which is reflected in the TTFT (Time to First Token). Our tests revealed that the timing differences caused by matching chunks are significant enough to be recognized and exploited.\n\nFor instance, if the victim has submitted a sensitive prompt or if a valuable system prompt has been cached, an attacker sharing the same backend could attempt to guess the victim's input. By measuring the TTFT based on prefix matches, the attacker could verify ",
                "text_truncated": true
              },
              {
                "citation": {
                  "chunk_id": "e81bf0e200a9664b5e131551",
                  "event_id": "1868db90-f569-426c-96d9-bf691a25088d",
                  "revision_id": "249beafe-bec3-4494-ab09-406fd96b85fd",
                  "revision": 1,
                  "raw_document_id": "6e452537-d696-4fa9-94c4-7ca8a27b2142",
                  "json_pointer": "/8/description",
                  "sha256": "97d404bc94da3609cd843125df913275f94cde6ba33f83e5175be86a3986665a",
                  "field": "description",
                  "start": 2340,
                  "end": 2502,
                  "external_id": "GHSA-4qjh-9fv9-r85r",
                  "source_id": "github-advisories",
                  "origin": "live"
                },
                "text": "tly to 0.99, showing that the attacker can almost perfectly identify cache hits with a longer prefix.\n\n## Fixes\n\n* https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/17045",
                "text_truncated": false
              },
